It's a space that's completely blown up, and through that,

32
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.360
we'll kind of distill the primitives that have proven

33
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.680
to be really useful, some stuff that has been a little bit less useful.

34
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.000
Secondly, we'll talk about

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.240
what the state of the art is for memory management today.

36
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.600
And thirdly, and in particular with that, we'll talk about not just what

37
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.960
nice theoretical principles are,

38
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.280
but what it takes to actually build these systems in production.

39
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.120
And then finally, we'll talk about where this will go on the path to continual

40
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.160
learning, and in particular, touching on a paradigm called dreaming.

41
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.280
So we said this before.

42
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.280
And models we release new models all the time.

43
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.240
They are more and more intelligent.

44
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.520
But when it comes to actually deploying these models in your agents,

45
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.520
in your environments, in your organization,

46
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.800
the intelligence alone is not going to compound because they need this context

47
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.800
that helps them perform the specific tasks that you need them to.

48
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.920
In particular, a lot of this context is often

49
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.400
kind of orthogonal to the model intelligence, right?

50
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.800
Like the newest model we've just released, one isn't going to

51
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.960
isn't going to out of the box, know exactly what it takes to succeed

52
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.000
in your organization and with the tasks that you want them to.

53
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.840
And so it's a really great investment to work on the context engineering part,

54
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.560
because this over time has the effect of multiplying

55
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.560
the intelligence even as models get smarter.

56
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.600
So I'm sure you'll all be familiar with these problems.

57
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.640
It's like agents not knowing their way around a code base, or

58
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.640
knowing enough about your own user preferences.

59
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.480
And then additionally, like you don't have the effect

60
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.280
where agents are better at the task the next time they perform it, so

61
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.320
they might not learn from their mistakes.

62
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.080
And as such, you don't have this continual learning effect.

63
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.520
So just to recap.

64
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.720
Where we've got so far on this journey of context

65
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.680
engineering, at Anthropic, we like to say do the simple thing that works.

66
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.680
And this is a timeline that only really spans the past year.

67
00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.640
And where we started

68
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.040
was with these CLAUDE.md files that we launched with Claude Code.

69
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.800
And what we learned from this was that it was kind of unreasonably effective,

70
00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.200
like this markdown file that just gives the agent

71
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.280
a couple of instructions about maybe your way around the code

72
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.600
base, the organization, your own user preferences

73
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.800
that injected into the beginning of the model's context

74
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.800
at the beginning of a session was so good

75
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.120
at steering it towards the things that mattered, and helping it navigate

76
00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.480
and align its actions towards your preferences.

77
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.200
However, we also learned a couple of things about what doesn't work here.

78
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.080
So when we're injecting this at the beginning,

79
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.920
at the beginning of the session into context, we obviously start

80
00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:38.800
to run into problems where you get problems with context,

81
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.880
like what happens when this file with very important preferences gets very,

82
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.720
very long. How do I manage that over time?

83
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:47.640
And so we

84
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.120
went back to the drawing board and thought about

85
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.720
ways that we could improve this separately, though what was true

86
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.440
was that having just a very simple markdown file,

87
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.040
which is human readable, which your agent can write, to, which you can write

88
00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.080
to, is really, really effective.

89
00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.440
So a second avenue that we investigated was these memory tools.

90
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:12.080
And this is interesting because it leans into this idea of okay,

91
00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:17.320
what happens if we let agents autonomously manage their own memory systems.

92
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.680
So we let them decide

93
00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.320
when they read, when they write and when they update memories.

94
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.040
And this is all happening in band, which means that it's

95
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.480
within the context of a session.

96
00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.360
So during a session, agent is thinking about like

97
00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.560
what might be interesting to pull from memory,

98
00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.440
what might be interesting to write to memory.

99
00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.600
So autonomy proves to work really well in this case.

100
00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.800
And over time,

101
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.800
we've kind of developed that into systems where we're even less opinionated

102
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.240
about what these tools need to look like, and I'll touch on that in a second.

103
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.280
So the next the next stop on this

104
00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.560
journey was skills, which I'm sure you've had a bunch about today.

105
00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:01.840
And what this solves is this problem of the ever growing context.

106
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.360
So we have this really clever idea of progressive disclosure.

107
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.880
The way I like to think about it is actually first on

108
00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.440
what skills are good at it's processes where you have like a procedural workflow.

109
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.240
So something where you have an opinion about how the process works end to end

110
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.920
that you want the agent to follow.

111
00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.720
And what's very clever is that the agent only looks at this front matter

112
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.320
a couple of sentences at the top of the file before loading the skill,

113
00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.040
but you can still load as much detail as you want

114
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.720
into the main body of the file, so you're able to, at the same time,

115
00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.840
have very deep levels of detail whilst not overloading the model's context.

116
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.440
And the way I like to think about it is as if I had a bookshelf in my room

117
00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.360
and every time someone talks to me, I can kind of scan

118
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.040
and look at my list of books and see if any of the titles

119
00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.080
might be relevant to the conversation, and kind of pick that off the shelf

120
00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.280
and read it when I need to.

121
00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.440
So, for example,

122
00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.920
if someone walked up to me and started speaking to me in French

123
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.880
and I noticed that I have a French dictionary,

124
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.440
I could pull that out and it would give me context

125
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.640
kind of loaded during the conversation that would help me without me

126
00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:09.840
having paid attention

127
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.560
in like seven years of French classes at school

128
00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:13.840
and having that all loaded into my context already.

129
00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.400
So this was a really, really great innovation.

130
00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.600
But one bottleneck potentially with it is

131
00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.360
that it's still kind of driven by humans and agents together.

132
00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.960
So you're still even if you're using your agents to write the skills with you.

133
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.480
You're still being quite opinionated about what things need skills.

134
00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.480
So that takes me to the final step on this path,

135
00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.400
which is what we perceive to be state of art for memory systems today,

136
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.800
and what we have done and what we think is best

137
00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.480
practice is modeling these memory systems just as file systems.

138
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.880
So this kind of aggregates a couple of the learnings from this path.

139
00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.200
So file systems are great.

140
00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.600
You can just fill them up with markdown.

141
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.800
Agents are actually just very good at using normal file system tools

142
00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:00.840
like bash and grep.

143
00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.040
So just let them search over the file system rather than being opinionated

144
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.440
about these specific tools that they should use to read and write to memory.

145
00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.880
And then, yeah, that search kind of mirrors

146
00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.800
this idea of progressive disclosure.

147
00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.520
You can index these memory systems really well so that agents can intelligently

148
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:18.400
search over them.

149
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.840
And that's where we have kind of got to so far.

150
00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.640
So just to

151
00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.200
recap the key linings from that format.

152
00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.200
Markdown is great for reading memories.

153
00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.400
You know, allow the memories to grow large, but

154
00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.920
give agents tools to quickly index and search for what's relevant,

155
00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:41.400
and finally give agents autonomy when they're writing to memories.

156
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.240
And if you were to go out and build a system, it would work really well.

157
00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.880
You would have the feeling of continual learning, because your agents

158
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.400
would get better at the individual, whatever individual tasks you're doing.

159
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:56.960
However,

160
00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.480
as with everything, this very neat idea

161
00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.040
runs into some problems when you try and scale it to production.

162
00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.960
So we have and we have a concept for theoretically what works.

163
00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.080
And when.

164
00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:13.400
We then think about scaling these to production in environments

165
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:17.360
where we have many agents collaborating at the same time, where they run over

166
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:22.200
very long periods of time, and where potentially

167
00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.200
these codebases get really complicated, all manner of problems start to arise.

168
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.040
And we've seen these in production time and time again.

169
00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.080
So one, a couple of things just to like spark your imagination.

170
00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.240
Think about multiple agents trying to write to a memory file at the same time.

171
00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:38.680
How do you manage that?

172
00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.320
Think about one agent running into a problem

173
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:45.680
and deciding to write to the organizational wide context

174
00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.760
which every other agent is currently reading from.

175
00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.880
Like if something was incorrect there, that would scale

176
00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:53.920
to all of your agents and be pretty disastrous.

177
00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.640
And think about when you have humans and agents collaborating on on memory

178
00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.880
contexts together, like how do you keep track of what's going on?

179
00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.000
The final problem is that memories can go stale.

180
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.440
Of course, something that was relevant in the past might not be relevant today.

181
00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.920
Or maybe it was written incorrectly or even maliciously injected

182
00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.600
by someone trying to prompt inject your agents to write bad things to memory.

183
00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.960
So you have to have a lot of guardrails in place to make sure that these nice

184
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.040
autonomous memory systems actually work in production.

185
00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:33.240
And so I'm going to talk through a couple of key principles

186
00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:37.560
that we use when designing memory systems in production to make sure that we do

187
00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.680
get to use all of those nice effects that we've talked, that we've spoken about.

188
00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.200
So the very first thing is versioning.

189
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.480
So when you're designing

190
00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.880
any kind of memory system, you need to be able to store versions

191
00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:53.760
to keep track of what's going on, to allow you to roll back

192
00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:57.320
should you need to, if a new update isn't particularly good.

193
00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:58.920
Additionally,

194
00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.680
you probably want to think about what context was this update based on.

195
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.000
So which agent session?

196
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.080
Which transcript resulted in me wanting to make this update?

197
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.240
And additionally,

198
00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.280
like you might want to track like who did it, which agent, which human, etc.

199
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:15.640
etc..

200
00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.160
So this is really important.

201
00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.480
The second thing is concurrency.

202
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.640
So we've talked about okay, what happens when I deploy thousands of agents

203
00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.640
all working off the same memory system.

204
00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.240
And the solution that we've adopted

205
00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.280
here is to have this hashing system.

206
00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.600
So when an agent decides that it wants to write

207
00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.600
an update to a memory, it takes a hash.

208
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.280
It then drafts its edit.

209
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.560
And then before it writes the update, it takes another hash.

210
00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.960
If those two things do not match, then the agent cannot write it

211
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.160
because it means that some update was made in the meantime.

212
00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:53.120
And in order to handle that, the agent ripples

213
00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:57.040
the memory, drafts its new update, and then tries to commit this again.

214
00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:01.000
So these are the kinds of just engineering practices that allow you to scale

215
00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.880
multiple agent architectures, scale memory to these kinds of architectures.

216
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.240
Another couple key principles.

217
00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.240
So permissions is really important.

218
00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.600
When you have large memory bases

219
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.800
you probably have a mixture of top level organizational wide knowledge.

220
00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.880
It might be like your key like what your organization is trying to achieve,

221
00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:30.800
or key principles about the code base, which you've really carefully curated

222
00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.680
all the way down to the level of like a scratchpad for an agent,

223
00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.680
where it writes down its working memory

224
00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:40.520
and it's very individualized and all the way in between.

225
00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.640
You can have things for specific organizations or cross sections.

226
00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.040
And so it's really important that you have guardrails

227
00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.520
when it comes to permissions, these memories.

228
00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:53.640
So like I said, you wouldn't want one agent to just decide that

229
00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.720
it should update the organization wide context.

230
00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.760
Probably you might want that as read only.

231
00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.280
However for its own scratchpad, you would want it to have right access

232
00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.600
and yeah.

233
00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.640
Yeah that's that's permissions.

234
00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:10.640
The final thing which is kind of peripheral

235
00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:15.720
but still really important when you design memory systems is portability.

236
00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.920
So like I mentioned

237
00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:22.000
before, your curation of your memory

238
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.080
systems is going to be so important like throughout the future.

239
00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.520
This is really really important organization user

240
00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.680
or like work task specific context.

241
00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:36.360
And so it's likely that if you're putting a lot of effort into curating this,

242
00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:40.880
you want it to be accessible across potentially multiple product surfaces

243
00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:45.240
and access accessible by multiple systems.

244
00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:48.280
So designing it in a way with a clean API in which it's portable

245
00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.280
and you can access it is really important.

246
00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.040
And so when you put all these things together,

247
00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:58.760
we have the kind of learnings we have from allowing agents

248
00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:03.160
to creatively manage their memory, and then these production level guardrails

249
00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:07.360
that allow them to reasonably use all of those principles in practice.

250
00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.880
And when you do this, you get very effective results.

251
00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:14.720
So just sharing here a couple of learnings from what we've seen,

252
00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:18.680
deploying these large scale memory systems in production.

253
00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.600
And for example we see you get better accuracy.

254
00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.320
So you have this effect where the second time the agent does

255
00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.080
the task, it actually does it better with higher results,

256
00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:34.600
because it's noted all of those memories about what went wrong.

257
00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.440
Secondly, that then has second order effect on the speed and latency, sorry,

258
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:43.440
speed and cost of your agents running because then spending

259
00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:47.560
fewer tokens, they can be more easily one shot to these tasks because

260
00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.640
they actually know what they're doing.

261
00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.360
And you'll see that across all sorts of different processes,

262
00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.560
agents are just able to do the task better and faster.

263
00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.000
Finally, having this process where your agents are starting

264
00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:04.320
to autonomously write their own memories frees up capacity

265
00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:08.640
and context for you as product developers, potentially to focus on product wins.

266
00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:10.120
While you know that the agents are doing

267
00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.920
this kind of self-learning continual learning loop in the background,

268
00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.000
and once that infrastructure is set up really well,

269
00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.080
this this works very symbiotically.

270
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:24.480
As ever.

271
00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.080
We do then reach a new bottleneck.

272
00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:30.920
And this specifically is about in-band memory.

273
00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:35.800
So in-band memory as I mentioned before, is when agents are writing to

274
00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.520
and reading from memory within a specific session.

275
00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.800
So if you think about Claude code for when you spin up a new session,

276
00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.960
it's it's largely like focusing on that specific context

277
00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.960
when it's reading to reading and writing from memory.

278
00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:56.200
And this just architecturally or philosophically has limitations

279
00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.760
in the general like agent fleet's continual learning objectives.

280
00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.680
There's two, two main reasons why, first of all, is

281
00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.800
that you have this inherent split of focus and resources.

282
00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.120
So you're asking an agent to complete a task, but at the same time,

283
00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:15.200
you're also asking it to invest in memory curation, which would help it

284
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.920
perform better in a future run.

285
00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:20.480
So when you put these things together, it's just a very difficult

286
00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:21.520
optimization problem.

287
00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:24.560
Because how much capacity should an agent put into helping future

288
00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:28.440
versions of itself versus doing the task that you actually asked it to do?

289
00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:30.240
And also

290
00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.240
there's like other effects like latency, for example.

291
00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:35.600
The second thing is that

292
00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.880
the agents just have an inherent visibility limitation.

293
00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:43.600
So they only have the context of what's going on in their session.

294
00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:46.680
As such, they just won't see patterns that happen across sessions.

295
00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.120
So when you get frustrated that your agent keeps making the same mistake over

296
00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:53.440
sessions, it just doesn't understand how frustrating that is because

297
00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.480
it has a new context window in each of those.

298
00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:57.440
Secondly, when you're running

299
00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.840
multiple fleets of agents in different environments, you

300
00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:02.840
these single agents just don't have the context

301
00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:05.840
of what other failures other agents are running into.

302
00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:09.600
So for these two reasons, we introduce this concept of some out-of-band

303
00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.040
memory curation.

304
00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:14.520
And this helps to make these problems go away.

305
00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:19.640
And just to introduce an analogy for why this in theory should work,

306
00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.880
I'd like you to think about a school, for example,

307
00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:25.560
where you have lots of students that submit a lot of work,

308
00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:28.520
and you also have teachers

309
00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.520
at market and a head teacher that reviews everything.

310
00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.520
This is a system that we have in the real world for good reason.

311
00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:39.280
And it's because when you have certain individuals that have dedicated capacity

312
00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.720
for helping people learn, that's really effective.

313
00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:43.120
And when you also have people

314
00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.640
that have visibility over the whole fleet of agents or learners,

315
00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.600
and they're able to spot patterns and then kind of steer context

316
00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.640
or let's say in this case, the curriculum that's also really effective.

317
00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.280
So as always,

318
00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:59.280
we kind of look to the real world world to think about how to build these systems.

319
00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:01.640
Sorry, I also didn't

320
00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:04.680
touch on a final limitation, which is that memories go stale.

321
00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.680
So you need something some pass that checks that

322
00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:10.840
everything that's written there is still correct.

323
00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:15.760
And so we introduced this concept of dreaming,

324
00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:19.680
which is like a second second order process over memory.

325
00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.960
So if we think about how that's been constructed we have the like

326
00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.760
actual context which agents reference and has useful information,

327
00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:32.040
the memory processes which allow agents to kind of autonomously manage

328
00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.280
that context themselves.

329
00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:39.080
And then dreaming, which is a process that runs in batch and asynchronously

330
00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:43.840
with its own allocated resources to ensure that those memories themselves

331
00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:47.040
are effective up to date, and helping the agents

332
00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.040
learn over time.

333
00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:54.840
So what does dreaming look like?

334
00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.000
Essentially, what

335
00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.840
we do is we take an existing memory store.

336
00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.840
So this is a collection of memories.

337
00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:07.680
We then take a bunch of sessions or transcripts

338
00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:10.680
from agent interactions over a period of time.

339
00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:16.320
And we give these together to an agent, which reviews all of the transcripts,

340
00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:20.440
looks at the memory store, and starts to identify patterns

341
00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.800
for where there could be uplift in the memories.

342
00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.760
It then outputs a new memory store,

343
00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.080
whether it proposed changes to the existing memory store

344
00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:36.000
and what the agent is able to do, as I mentioned, is

345
00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:39.960
spend tokens on solving this problem of making agents learn better.

346
00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:44.400
Identify patterns for where agents are consistently failing,

347
00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:48.880
and then proposed changes for what might make a more effective memory store,

348
00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:51.920
such that next day when you run these agents again,

349
00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.520
they're actually feel smarter and they're running better.

350
00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:56.880
To go back to my analogy,

351
00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.240
just to paint some pictures of like what this could look like in practice,

352
00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:04.960
let's imagine that the head teacher reviewing all these transcripts

353
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.920
notices that every geography student has incorrectly answered a question.

354
00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:11.880
They're just all writing like complete garbage.

355
00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.040
To this question, the teacher notices that

356
00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:18.040
actually by kind of in this case,

357
00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:21.840
analyzing the memory store, that entire topic is missing from the curriculum.

358
00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.840
So while the teacher is able to do is notice that pattern,

359
00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.440
look at the memory store and suggest a new change to that curriculum

360
00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.640
such that the next day when these agents run,

361
00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.640
they now have that information that they needed.

362
00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:36.680
To give another example, the teacher might notice

363
00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:40.320
that in a certain maths exam, all of the answers are wrong.

364
00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:43.480
In the same way, all of the students are outputting radians

365
00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:45.160
when it's meant to be degrees.

366
00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:48.440
I don't know if anyone else in like GCSE maths had that problem too,

367
00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:52.240
but what they're able to do is give an instruction saying like,

368
00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.240
this is how you should configure your calculators.

369
00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:59.200
And in the case of agents, that's like noticing in the transcripts

370
00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:01.280
that there's something wrong with the tool configuration.

371
00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:04.840
So you might notice that something in the tool calls keeps failing.

372
00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:08.760
And what's important here is that when we look at those transcripts,

373
00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:13.160
we are not just looking at kind of the passes of like responses

374
00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:16.280
between agent and the system or the user.

375
00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:20.400
We're also really scrutinizing those tool calls and all of the other metadata

376
00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:23.120
that is really central to the agent's performance.

377
00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:25.840
Finally, you could also

378
00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.880
notice something that's like fleet wide or organization wide.

379
00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:32.440
So for example, like everybody is using too many dashes and you don't like that.

380
00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:36.160
So you want to add some organizational wide

381
00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:40.360
announcement or context change that says not to do that.

382
00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:44.680
And so I hope that paints a picture of why this could be really, really effective.

383
00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:47.960
And now I'll just talk about

384
00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:53.120
how you would go about designing such a system in production.

385
00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:57.000
So you have some concept of your memory store,

386
00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.000
which is a concept which is a collection of memories.

387
00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:04.520
Memories themselves might just be marked down files organized in this directory.

388
00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.520
You then take a number of the transcripts.

389
00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:13.360
And like I mentioned, that is a mixture of like the back and forth passes

390
00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:18.520
between the agents as well as metadata on tools, other skills they used, etc.

391
00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.800
and the way that we've designed it, we have the orchestrator deploy

392
00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.640
a fleet of subjects that go and analyze all those transcripts.

393
00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:28.480
And one point

394
00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:31.640
worth making here is that when you design these systems,

395
00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.520
you have the ability to steer how these agents,

396
00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.360
which both write memories and coordinate dreaming, go about the problem.

397
00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:42.760
And by steering I mean that you're able to tell them, like

398
00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:45.120
in your specific case, these are the kinds of things

399
00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:46.800
I think are important and relevant.

400
00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.240
These are the kinds of things that are not important and relevant.

401
00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:53.000
So you do have the ability there to start to curate that memory

402
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:57.000
and dreaming process to your organization specifically.

403
00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.400
And the orchestrator

404
00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:03.400
then reviews all of the responses from the subjects.

405
00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:07.680
And it then decides like where there are prevalent enough

406
00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:11.760
patterns, that it thinks this warrants a change in the memories.

407
00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:16.200
From there, it proposes individual changes to the memory store

408
00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:20.480
and in our case, the way that we've designed this in production is

409
00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:24.480
the agent will additionally give you of transcripts

410
00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:26.280
where it's noticed this pattern has happened,

411
00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:29.320
and also some stats on like how prevalent this issue is

412
00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:32.800
and why this warrants actually updating the memory store.

413
00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.000
And so all of this works really neatly.

414
00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:42.520
You get this output and you as the individual can decide where

415
00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:47.200
you want to accept changes to the memory, where you want to reject them, etc.

416
00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:50.400
and this works really effectively.

417
00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:53.920
So together

418
00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:56.920
we have these two processes that run in parallel.

419
00:23:58.440 --> 00:23:59.880
The first is memory.

420
00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:03.800
And these agents are using

421
00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:07.520
some of their like in-band context

422
00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:11.040
and in-band resources to write to memory where they think it's important.

423
00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.360
And this is neat because it means that in the actual

424
00:24:14.360 --> 00:24:17.560
next run, the next session, that agent will be better.

425
00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:20.280
So there's a shorter time to kind of seeing that change.

426
00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:23.600
But inherently these agents have competing resources

427
00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:26.800
when they think about what to dedicate to memory, what to dedicate

428
00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:31.040
to completing the task, and additionally a lack of visibility.

429
00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:36.480
So on the other side, we have dreaming, which is this out-of-band process.

430
00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:39.600
And this allows, again broader

431
00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:42.800
visibility and dedicated capacity, i.e.

432
00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:46.280
token spend, which is specifically directed towards helping agents

433
00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:48.280
learn better.

434
00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:51.520
And you might think, okay, that sounds really expensive.

435
00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.560
Why would I want to chuck extra resources at this?

436
00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:58.080
But if we kind of go back to the improvements we saw when you build

437
00:24:58.080 --> 00:25:02.360
effective memory stores, actually you can see a bunch of costs go down

438
00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.240
because agents are able to one shot things more effectively.

439
00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:10.840
They have more information that they need in order to perform a task well.

440
00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:17.160
So to summarize.

441
00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.360
At the very least, do the simple thing that works.

442
00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:26.680
Context management makes such a huge difference to your agent performance.

443
00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:31.680
Implementing things like a CLAUDE.md file like skills,

444
00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.280
which I'm sure you've heard about a bunch, and allowing agents

445
00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:37.600
to autonomously manage these systems themselves goes a really long way.

446
00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:42.360
Once you think about scaling those things into architectures

447
00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:46.120
with many agents, agents that run over a very long time,

448
00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:51.120
situations where you will continue to work and develop

449
00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:54.160
on a workspace or code base over a long period of time

450
00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:57.760
or very complex domains, you should start thinking

451
00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:01.360
about adding some features or some guardrails that allow those agents

452
00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:05.640
to manage their memory in a way that is safe, verifiable, auditable.

453
00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:09.760
I should also say here

454
00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:13.360
that whilst this kind of these kinds of practices

455
00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.480
are really effective when it comes to coding

456
00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:19.000
task, for example, this by no means is just specific to coding.

457
00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:22.400
Like I use memory all the time when I'm producing presentations.

458
00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:26.400
It has context on like how I like to write things, how I like my slides, etc. etc.

459
00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:27.520
and that develops over time.

460
00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:30.440
So this is really not coding specific.

461
00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:33.320
The final thing is

462
00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:35.840
if you really want to kind of close the loop here.

463
00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:40.840
Think about adding an additional out of band process like dreaming as we call it,

464
00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:45.800
to consolidate your memory and cut things that are no longer relevant.

465
00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:50.200
Add things that agents are missing and clean up and organize memory systems.

466
00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:52.920
So to

467
00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:57.760
close, I want to say that this journey that we've been on with context

468
00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:01.760
engineering, a lot of this stuff has only happened in this past year.

469
00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.480
This is very much an open area of research

470
00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:08.400
and development, and one in which we see huge value in the future.

471
00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:10.880
So we're so excited to see the kinds of things

472
00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:13.440
and contributions that you all make to this space.

473
00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:17.280
So I encourage you to keep thinking, keep learning and keep dreaming.

474
00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:20.320
Thank you.

475
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:26.880
Thank you.

476
00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:28.480
That was great.

477
00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:30.560
Do we have any questions in the room?

478
00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:31.600
Oh, golly gosh.

479
00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:34.600
I'll dive straight here first.

480
00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.120
Thank you.

481
00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:38.160
Thanks for the presentation.

482
00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:43.280
Do you have any memory store implementation that you would like

483
00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:44.920
to suggest?

484
00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:49.560
Any any memory storage implementation that you would like us?

485
00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:49.760
Sorry.

486
00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:51.800
Did you say. Papers or no.

487
00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:53.600
Memory storage implementation?

488
00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.440
Okay. Okay. That we'd like. To suggest.

489
00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.160
To solve what problems specifically?

490
00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:01.120
Well, one thing

491
00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:04.120
is put things in files that I have on my laptop,

492
00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:08.320
but I think I'm looking into something more enterprise.

493
00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:12.560
So what kind of solution do you suggest over there?

494
00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:14.240
Okay. Yeah. So.

